Atoms contain positively charged nuclei and negatively charged electrons. Figure 130 retains the classical orbit illustration used by the source for hydrogen: a positively charged nucleus is at the center, with an electron shown around it. Modern quantum mechanics does not interpret such an orbit drawing as a definite electron path;
it describes the electron with a probability distribution. For ground-state hydrogen with no applied field, the average electron charge distribution is spherically symmetric, and its center coincides with the nucleus. To explain time averaging, the source gives an orbit-period order of $10^{-16}$ seconds and an observation interval of $10^{-9}$ seconds. It then says a million revolutions, with a million visits to each of the symmetric points 1 and 2. Treating those two times as exact values and dividing them does not give the stated count;
these historical estimates are not evidence of a modern electron trajectory. The dashed marks in Figure 131 retain the series of electron positions in the old schematic, not a measured trajectory. The relevant idea is that the positive and negative charge centers coincide, not an orbit-count calculation.

